{"alignments":[{"id":"alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083","layer":"CN","nodeId":"cnm_h091","reason":"The cosine law and cosine 0 or 1/2 computations are shared. Canonical criteria exclude cosine 1 for an included angle of a nondegenerate triangle, an invalid source case.","relation":"partial","reviewProvenance":{"effort":"medium","evidenceFile":"cn-refined-review.json","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","sourceRevision":"62b341d4b66b175413d6e3f697e37639fd96aa2d","type":"initial machine content review, not teacher review or official equivalence"},"reviewStatus":"machine_content_review","sourceNodeId":"cnm_h091","status":"accepted"}],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 84","reason":"Set u=AD, v=CD, w=BC. The geometry gives w²=u²+(18-v)²-u(18-v). For d>0, assign u=18-id, v=18-jd, w=18-kd over the six permutations of (1,2,3). The resulting d values are 18,2,9,5,6,6; only 2 and 5 satisfy 0<d<6. These yield other-side lengths {12,14,16} and {3,8,13}. The constant progression d=0 also permits a=18. Their distinct values sum to 84.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-law-triangle","label":"Triangle with a 60-degree angle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-law-triangle":["cnm_h091"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"auxiliary-triangle","label":"Reduce to two triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-triangle":["cnm_h202","cnm_111","cnm_h091","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h202","cnm_111","cnm_h091","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (63/11)","reason":"The inscribed angles give ∠BAC=∠CAD=30°. Thus BD²=9²+24²−2·9·24·cos60°=441, so BD=21. Since BF:FD=9:24, BF=21·9/(9+24)=63/11.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-rule-diagonal","label":"Find BD by the cosine rule","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-rule-diagonal":["cnm_h091"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-law-angle-bisector","label":"Find the diagonal and divide it by a ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-law-angle-bisector":["cnm_253","cnm_h091","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_253","cnm_h091","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"7","reason":"c²=2²+3²-2×2×3×(1/2)=4+9-6=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h091_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_029","cnm_021"],"id":"m1","label":"Law of cosines","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h091"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_029","cnm_021"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D; -2ab cos C is negative and c²<a²+b²","reason":"Side lengths a,b are positive and an acute C has cos C>0. Thus -2ab cos C<0, and c²=a²+b²-2ab cos C<a²+b².","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h091_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_015","cnm_096"],"id":"m1","label":"Sign of the cosine correction","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h061","cnm_h091"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h061","cnm_h091","cnm_015","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 60°","reason":"The relation gives a²+2ab+b²-c²=3ab, so a²+b²-c²=ab. Therefore cos C=(a²+b²-c²)/(2ab)=1/2, and C=60°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"construct-special-triangle-and-sss","label":"Construct a 60-degree comparison triangle and use SSS","methodDirectNodeIds":{"construct-special-triangle-and-sss":["cnm_h091","cnm_122"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_122"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_149","cnm_h091","cnm_277"],"id":"recover-angle-by-cosine-law","label":"Reveal the cosine-law expression","methodDirectNodeIds":{"recover-angle-by-cosine-law":["math:general-cosine-law-application"]},"nodeIds":["math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_149","cnm_h091","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 17","reason":"Let t be the side adjacent to the 60° angle. Then (90-t)²=80²+t²-80t, giving 1700=100t and t=17. The sides are 17,73,80, so 17 is shortest.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q36","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-law-side-sum","label":"Use the cosine law","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-law-side-sum":["cnm_h091","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3072","reason":"Let x be Abdul-Bharat and y be Bharat-Chiang. The cosine law gives 48²=x²+y²-xy=(y-x/2)²+3x²/4. Thus x²≤3072, with equality when y=x/2, an attainable right-triangle configuration.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-square-bound","label":"Use the cosine law and complete a square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-square-bound":["cnm_h091","math:completing-square-expression"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","math:completing-square-expression"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"25","reason":"The third side is the hypotenuse, so c²=3²+4²=9+16=25.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h091_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_021"],"id":"m1","label":"Zero cosine correction","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h091"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_021"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 2","reason":"The cosine rule gives BC²=3²+6²−2·3·6 cos120°=63. The bisector theorem gives CD:DB=1:2, so CD·DB=14. Stewart's relation then gives AD²=3·6−14=4, hence AD=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bisector-and-special-cosines","label":"Use the bisector ratio and the two supplied 60-degree angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bisector-and-special-cosines":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_h091","cnm_036","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_h091","cnm_036","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 7√21/20","reason":"The triangle has side length 3. Put s=AP and t=AQ. Reflection gives PA'=s and QA'=t. The cosine rule in BPA' gives s²=(3−s)²+1−(3−s), hence s=7/5. Similarly, t²=(3−t)²+4−2(3−t), giving t=7/4. Therefore PQ²=s²+t²−st=1029/400, so PQ=7√21/20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-distances-and-cosine-rule","label":"Use fold distances and cosine rule","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-distances-and-cosine-rule":["cnm_h091","cnm_042","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_042","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 90°","reason":"Let α = ∠BAE = ∠ACD. Since ∠AEB = 120°, ∠ABC = 60° - α, while ∠ACB = 60° + α. Hence ∠BAC = 60°. Writing AC = s and AB = 2s, the cosine law gives BC² = 3s². Therefore AC² + BC² = AB², so ∠ACB = 90°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-law-and-pythagorean-converse","label":"Cosine law and Pythagorean converse","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-law-and-pythagorean-converse":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 90 degrees","reason":"Put alpha=angle ACD=angle BAE. The equilateral triangle gives angle ACE=60+alpha and angle AEC=60, so angle CAE=60-alpha and angle BAC=60. Writing AC=s and AB=2s, the cosine rule gives BC^2=4s^2+s^2-2(2s)(s)(1/2)=3s^2. Hence AB^2=AC^2+BC^2, so angle ACB=90 degrees.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-and-pythagorean-converse","label":"Cosine rule and right-triangle test","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-and-pythagorean-converse":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_177"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 73","reason":"PA=18 and PC(PC+7)=8·18=144, giving PC=9. The cosine rule gives AC²=243, so PA²=PC²+AC² and AC is perpendicular to CD. Thus AD is a diameter, and r²=(243+49)/4=73.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"right-angle-diameter","label":"Identify a diameter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-angle-diameter":["cnm_h091","cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_177","cnm_254","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: sqrt(3)/(4sqrt(2)-3)","reason":"For t=CA>0, cosine-law and area relationships give tan(BAD)=sqrt(3)/(t+8/t-3). Since t+8/t>=4sqrt(2), with equality at t=2sqrt(2), the largest value is sqrt(3)/(4sqrt(2)-3).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"special-cosine-dot-product-amgm","label":"Special 60-degree side calculation, dot product, and AM-GM","methodDirectNodeIds":{"special-cosine-dot-product-amgm":["cnm_h091","cnm_h088","cnm_p148","cnm_h022"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_h088","cnm_p148","cnm_h022"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_h021"],"id":"cosine-area-amgm","label":"Express the tangent using one side length","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-area-amgm":["cnm_h091","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_p148","cnm_h022"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_p148","cnm_h022","cnm_055","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_h021"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (36)","reason":"If 60° is opposite x, 5, or 4, respectively, the cosine law gives x^2=21, x^2-4x-9=0, or x^2-5x+9=0. The valid lengths are sqrt(21) and 2+sqrt(13); the last equation has no real root. Thus a+b+c=2+21+13=36.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-law-cases","label":"Use the cosine law","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-law-cases":["cnm_h091","cnm_220","cnm_221"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h091","cnm_220","cnm_221"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 552","reason":"For the shown orientation, adjacent centers have separation 8, with components 6 and s−2√3 relative to a triangle side of length s. Thus (s−2√3)²+36=64, giving s=2(√3+√7). The area is (√3/4)s²=10√3+6√7=√300+√252, so a+b=552.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":["The shown large tangent configuration is the branch with area10sqrt(3)+6sqrt(7); the other positive branch has area10sqrt(3)-6sqrt(7)<2 and cannot satisfy the given sum of two positive integer square roots."],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"common-center-cosine-law","label":"Common center and cosine law","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-center-cosine-law":["cnm_245","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_h091","cnm_221"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_245","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_h091","cnm_221"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"alignmentIds":["alignment:602e3d7cb9ebffb68c0ea083"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, (10-6√2)/7","reason":"Let s=BN and r=AN/BN. Choose N=(0,0), B=(s,0), and X=(s/2,√3s/2). The midpoint condition gives C=((r+2)s/2,√3(r+2)s/2). Using AC/BC=r yields (r²-2)(r+1)(r+2)=0, so r=√2. Consequently 4=AC²=s²(10+6√2), and s²=4/(10+6√2)=(10-6√2)/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_p148","cnm_277"],"id":"reflection-and-median-areas","label":"Reflect across the bisector and compare areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reflection-and-median-areas":["cnm_113","cnm_139","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_132","cnm_h091","cnm_220","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_139","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_132","cnm_h091","cnm_220","cnm_171","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_p148","cnm_277"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h091"],"label":"余弦定理","layer":"CN","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"criteria":["能写出余弦定理","能在 cos 为 0、1 或 1/2 时算出第三边的平方"],"description":"会用余弦定理求第三边的平方，已知两边及其夹角的余弦。","id":"cnm_h091","label":"余弦定理","original":{"chapter":"高二 第六章 平面向量及其应用 6.4","description":"会用余弦定理求第三边的平方，已知两边及其夹角的余弦。","domain":"图形与几何","external":false,"grade":11,"id":"cnm_h091","mastery_criteria":["能写出余弦定理","能在 cos 为 0、1 或 1/2 时算出第三边的平方"],"name":"余弦定理","semester":"上","standards":[{"confidence":"exact","id":"cms-2017:H.GA.05"}],"type":"procedural"},"placement":{"bookCode":"11","bookLabel":"必修二","chapter":"高二 第六章 平面向量及其应用 6.4","domain":"图形与几何","semester":"上"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"cnm_h091","status":"partial","total":17}
